WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.033 --> 00:00:02.100
-Harry, it's great to see you.

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:03.667
And man, it's an honor to have
you here.

00:00:03.667 --> 00:00:05.167
-It's so cool to be here.

00:00:05.167 --> 00:00:07.868
It's the first time on "The
Tonight Show" in my whole life.

00:00:07.868 --> 00:00:09.067
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:09.067 --> 00:00:11.300
-I find that hard to believe.
-Yeah, right?

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:12.868
And oh, by the way, real quick.

00:00:12.868 --> 00:00:15.100
"Summer of Soul." Stop.
-Thank you.

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:18.200
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Just so great.

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:19.968
-Well done, Questlove. Yeah.

00:00:19.968 --> 00:00:23.234
Harry, we met, I would say,
20 years ago.

00:00:23.234 --> 00:00:24.300
I don't know
if you remember this.

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:27.267
You did an A&amp;E biography
on "SNL."

00:00:27.267 --> 00:00:29.634
-It was amazing.
We went behind the scenes.

00:00:29.634 --> 00:00:31.133
We shadowed the show for a week.

00:00:31.133 --> 00:00:32.667
Then we came back
and shot the thing.

00:00:32.667 --> 00:00:34.601
And that's when I first met you.
-Yeah.

00:00:34.601 --> 00:00:37.667
-And I have this very
crystal-clear memory.

00:00:37.667 --> 00:00:40.133
Tuesday night, they stay up
all night and write.

00:00:40.133 --> 00:00:42.400
-Yeah.
-And you and I are walking down

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:45.701
49th Street at like 4:30
in the morning.

00:00:45.701 --> 00:00:47.634
And I turned to you, and I said,

00:00:47.634 --> 00:00:49.400
"Where do you think this
all leads?

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:50.868
Where do you think this
all leads?"

00:00:50.868 --> 00:00:53.801
And back then, would you have
had any idea

00:00:53.801 --> 00:00:56.434
that you would be sitting
right here right now?

00:00:56.434 --> 00:00:57.534
-No.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:57.534 --> 00:00:59.534
No. No, never, never.

00:00:59.534 --> 00:01:00.968
-Right?
-Wow, no.

00:01:00.968 --> 00:01:02.968
-Yeah.
-Not never. Not at all.

00:01:02.968 --> 00:01:05.601
I didn't know this was even
a job that someone could have.

00:01:05.601 --> 00:01:07.534
[ Laughter ]
But I was just so psyched.

00:01:07.534 --> 00:01:09.334
I'm the biggest fan of yours.

00:01:09.334 --> 00:01:13.200
I said it backstage, but I'll
say it in front of stage.

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:16.033
Every time I see you, whether
it's a humorous piece you do,

00:01:16.033 --> 00:01:19.167
whether it's an emotional piece,
I love you and I have to --

00:01:19.167 --> 00:01:21.167
I can't tune out.
You're like when "The Godfather"

00:01:21.167 --> 00:01:24.801
comes on TV or "Love Actually,"
I have to watch the whole movie.

00:01:24.801 --> 00:01:26.200
-You can't change the channel.
-No, you can't.

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:28.133
I go, "Harry Smith, he's
the greatest," I mean --

00:01:28.133 --> 00:01:29.667
-Oh, you're
nice. You're too nice.

00:01:29.667 --> 00:01:31.934
-No, you're a pro.

00:01:31.934 --> 00:01:34.400
The recent thing you just did
with Al Pacino was awesome.

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:36.234
I thought that was great.

00:01:36.234 --> 00:01:39.334
-So the Tribeca Film Festival
screened "Heat"

00:01:39.334 --> 00:01:41.167
from 1995, great classic.

00:01:41.167 --> 00:01:43.501
Michael Mann film.

00:01:43.501 --> 00:01:46.200
And so they had Al Pacino
and Robert De Niro

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:48.467
and we got them backstage
for like 20 minutes.

00:01:48.467 --> 00:01:50.033
-Yeah.
-And --

00:01:50.033 --> 00:01:51.701
You have --
Do you have any experience

00:01:51.701 --> 00:01:53.934
with interviewing
Robert De Niro?

00:01:53.934 --> 00:01:57.901
-Robert De Niro, yes, he was
my first interview, by the way.

00:01:57.901 --> 00:01:59.133
He was my first guest
on "Late Night."

00:01:59.133 --> 00:02:01.534
-And how much did he say?
-He said two words.

00:02:01.534 --> 00:02:04.067
He said, "yep" and "nope."

00:02:04.067 --> 00:02:05.868
He's like, "Yep."

00:02:05.868 --> 00:02:09.133
-We got a couple of paragraphs
from him.

00:02:09.133 --> 00:02:11.133
-I mean, you did?
-'Cause I've interviewed him

00:02:11.133 --> 00:02:13.434
a bunch of times, and
honestly -- well, he was on

00:02:13.434 --> 00:02:15.100
the "Today" show a couple of
weeks ago

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:16.968
talking about
the Tribeca Film Festival.

00:02:16.968 --> 00:02:20.367
And here's Hoda and here's
Savannah and they're saying,

00:02:20.367 --> 00:02:23.701
"Bob, Bob, Bob."
"Ask Jane. Ask Jane. Ask Jane."

00:02:23.701 --> 00:02:27.367
Who runs it with him.
And it's just like --

00:02:27.367 --> 00:02:30.100
And so I do all this homework.
I do all this stuff.

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:33.300
And I just say, "Please, please,
talk to me."

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:34.534
And I got lucky.

00:02:34.534 --> 00:02:37.767
-Yeah, but you've been doing
this for 50 years?

00:02:37.767 --> 00:02:39.200
Is that true? Almost 50 years?

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:43.200
-So, I graduated college
in 1973.

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:46.133
I went to Central College
in Iowa to play football.

00:02:46.133 --> 00:02:48.767
But I messed around with
a college radio station,

00:02:48.767 --> 00:02:53.000
and I got a job doing
color commentary

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:55.067
on girls high school
basketball games

00:02:55.067 --> 00:02:57.701
at the local radio station,
daytime station.

00:02:57.701 --> 00:03:01.968
And the local veterinarian
and I would take his pickup

00:03:01.968 --> 00:03:05.767
out to see these girl high
school basketball games.

00:03:05.767 --> 00:03:08.167
And since it was only on during
the daytime,

00:03:08.167 --> 00:03:10.601
we're there at 6:00 at night.
It's not live.

00:03:10.601 --> 00:03:13.467
So we had a reel-to-reel
tape recorder,

00:03:13.467 --> 00:03:15.767
and we would record the games.

00:03:15.767 --> 00:03:19.167
The commercials I wrote for
D&amp;D Pump and Pipe, right?

00:03:19.167 --> 00:03:21.767
This is not a joke.
This is not a joke.

00:03:21.767 --> 00:03:24.167
[ Laughter ]
And they would replay them.

00:03:24.167 --> 00:03:26.367
Even though everyone knew
the results of the game,

00:03:26.367 --> 00:03:28.501
they would replay them
the next afternoon.

00:03:28.501 --> 00:03:31.267
-Wow. What, on like A.M. radio
or something?

00:03:31.267 --> 00:03:32.367
-A.M. radio, whatever.

00:03:32.367 --> 00:03:34.634
KNIA in Pella, Iowa.

00:03:34.634 --> 00:03:36.033
-Is that right?
-Yeah.

00:03:36.033 --> 00:03:37.367
With studios in Knoxville.

00:03:37.367 --> 00:03:39.000
-You're an award-winning
journalist.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:40.334
You've interviewed everybody,

00:03:40.334 --> 00:03:43.901
celebrities, royalty,
presidents.

00:03:43.901 --> 00:03:46.434
I mean, have you --
have you met the Queen?

00:03:46.434 --> 00:03:47.234
Have you met the Queen?

00:03:47.234 --> 00:03:48.734
-Yeah. Okay. So...

00:03:48.734 --> 00:03:50.968
[ Audience ohs ]

00:03:50.968 --> 00:03:53.634
She was on some tour. It's got
to be in the '90s, right?

00:03:53.634 --> 00:03:57.067
And there's some big event at
the British ambassador's house

00:03:57.067 --> 00:03:58.901
in Washington, D.C.,

00:03:58.901 --> 00:04:02.367
and you get the invitation
and just go,

00:04:02.367 --> 00:04:03.934
"This has to be a mistake."

00:04:03.934 --> 00:04:07.667
I get there and it really is
the royalty of Washington, D.C.

00:04:07.667 --> 00:04:08.968
And you get instructions.

00:04:08.968 --> 00:04:10.634
Do not address the Queen.

00:04:10.634 --> 00:04:14.100
Don't ask her a question.
Don't say boo to her.

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:17.601
So, I'm close to the front of
the line, and I just do

00:04:17.601 --> 00:04:20.834
the curtsy or the bow or
whatever you're supposed to do.

00:04:20.834 --> 00:04:22.033
-Yeah. Curtsy.

00:04:22.033 --> 00:04:24.200
-And I'm like, "I'm not happy
with this.

00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:27.067
It didn't work for me."
-Wow.

00:04:27.067 --> 00:04:29.801
-So I doubled back --
I double-dipped on the Queen.

00:04:29.801 --> 00:04:31.300
[ Laughter ]

00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:34.534
I go back to the back of
the line, right,

00:04:34.534 --> 00:04:36.901
and I put my hand out,
and I said --

00:04:36.901 --> 00:04:40.434
My co-host in the morning then,
Paula Zahn -- I said,

00:04:40.434 --> 00:04:44.100
"She knows your cousin or your
something or your something."

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:46.434
And prattle, prattle, prattle.

00:04:46.434 --> 00:04:48.767
And she says, "Oh, really?"

00:04:48.767 --> 00:04:52.801
We had a little conversation,
which no one else had.

00:04:52.801 --> 00:04:56.067
-You did it?
-And then -- And then all

00:04:56.067 --> 00:04:59.367
the VIPs of Washington, D.C.,
descended on me and said,

00:04:59.367 --> 00:05:01.300
"What were you talking to
the Queen about?"

00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:03.200
-Yeah! That's right!

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:04.968
You're like, "Yeah, I'll show
you how to double-dip."

00:05:04.968 --> 00:05:07.667
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:07.667 --> 00:05:09.400
Wow.
-Crushing it.

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:10.868
Crushing it with the Queen.

00:05:10.868 --> 00:05:13.601
-What are your favorite
interviews?

00:05:13.601 --> 00:05:15.734
-Real people. Real people.

00:05:15.734 --> 00:05:18.300
Because honestly, I've
interviewed all the presidents.

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:19.801
I've interviewed everybody,
right?

00:05:19.801 --> 00:05:24.767
And most of my job, we just go
out into America and I know

00:05:24.767 --> 00:05:28.100
if we're paying attention to the
news every day, which we should,

00:05:28.100 --> 00:05:31.634
it feels like we're at each
other's necks constantly.

00:05:31.634 --> 00:05:34.467
-Yeah.
-It doesn't feel like that out
there, right?

00:05:34.467 --> 00:05:39.000
There are people out there
who are building community,

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:41.801
helping their neighbors,
they're loving their neighbors.

00:05:41.801 --> 00:05:46.234
That's what it feels like a lot
out there where I like to be.

00:05:46.234 --> 00:05:47.467
I've done stories --

00:05:47.467 --> 00:05:49.534
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:49.534 --> 00:05:51.767
-Yeah.
-Right?

00:05:51.767 --> 00:05:53.801
I've done stories
in all 50 states.

00:05:53.801 --> 00:05:56.767
I've done from every continent,
including Antarctica,

00:05:56.767 --> 00:06:00.901
and my pleasure zone is
out on the road,

00:06:00.901 --> 00:06:02.868
out talking to folks.

00:06:02.868 --> 00:06:04.334
-Harry Smith, you're a legend.

00:06:04.334 --> 00:06:08.000
I love that you're on the show.
Please come back any time.

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:09.801
-This is what happens --

00:06:09.801 --> 00:06:12.434
You know the segment
"Thank You Notes"?

00:06:12.434 --> 00:06:14.067
I got a thank-you note.

00:06:14.067 --> 00:06:16.801
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:16.801 --> 00:06:18.934
-You're the best. Harry Smith.

00:06:18.934 --> 00:06:21.133
You can see his reports
on the "Today" show,

00:06:21.133 --> 00:06:24.267
"NBC Nightly News with Lester
Holt" and "NBC News Now."

00:06:24.267 --> 00:06:26.300
Conan Gray performs next.
Stick around.

00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:28.300
Harry Smith
on "The Tonight Show."

